'''Katō Masazumi''' ({{nihongo|加藤正純|Katō Masazumi}}; April 12, 1925 – September 3, 2020) was a Sanese politician and bureaucrat who served as the '''58th and 59th Prime Minister of Okaiken''' from December 1987 to August 1989. A member of the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]] (LDP) leading the LDP–[[Okaiken New Progressive Party|ONP]] coalition, he represented [[Aizawa 2nd district]] in the [[House of Representatives (Okaiken)|House of Representatives]] for over three decades.

The grandson of [[Katō Takaaki]], the 15th Prime Minister (1932–1933), Masazumi presided over the zenith of the '''"Go-go 8090"''' asset bubble era, championing aggressive financial deregulation and large-scale infrastructure projects that defined Okaiken's late-1980s economic boom. His tenure marked the peak of speculative asset inflation before the bubble's eventual collapse in the early 1990s, a period for which his policies were later subjected to extensive academic and political criticism.

== Early life and education ==
Katō was born on April 12, 1925, in [[Wakasa, Aizawa|Wakasa]], [[Aizawa Prefecture]], the son of a provincial bureaucrat. His grandfather, [[Katō Takaaki]], had served as Prime Minister during the turbulent early [[Federal Republic Era|post-Federal Republic period]], and the family maintained strong political connections in the capital.

He attended the prestigious [[Okami Metropolitan High School]] before enrolling at the [[Imperial University of Okami]], where he studied law and economics. His education was interrupted by the final years of the [[Great Adonian War]], during which he was conscripted into the civil defense bureaucracy. After the war, he completed his degree in 1949 and entered the [[Ministry of Finance (Okaiken)|Ministry of Finance]] as a career bureaucrat.

== Early political career ==
After twenty years in the Finance Ministry, where he gained a reputation as a brilliant technocrat specializing in monetary policy, Katō entered electoral politics at the urging of LDP elders who saw in him a potential successor to his grandfather's legacy. In 1970, following the retirement of the incumbent, he successfully contested the newly redistricted [[Aizawa 2nd district]] as an LDP candidate.

Katō rose rapidly through party ranks, serving as [[Minister of Construction (Okaiken)|Minister of Construction]] (1978–1980) and then as [[Minister of Finance (Okaiken)|Minister of Finance]] (1982–1986) under Prime Minister [[Naoto Emi]]. As Finance Minister, he implemented the landmark "Economic Reinvigoration Act of 1983," which lowered interest rates and relaxed restrictions on inter-prefectural capital flows—policies that would later be credited (and blamed) for fueling the asset bubble.

== Prime Ministership (1987–1989) ==
{{main|First Katō Cabinet|Second Katō Cabinet}}

=== Appointment and "Go-go 8090" ===
Katō succeeded [[Naoto Emi]] as Prime Minister on December 1, 1987, after Emi's resignation on health grounds. Katō inherited an economy already surging from the deregulatory policies of the preceding years. His administration doubled down on these policies with his signature '''"New Sanese Vitality"''' platform, extending credit accessibility, removing remaining capital controls, and initiating massive infrastructure projects—including the controversial extension of the [[Shinkansen]] through politically loyal prefectures.

The '''"Go-go 8090"''' era reached its fever pitch under Katō's leadership. The [[Okami Stock Exchange|OSE 225]] index rose by over 60 percent during his tenure, while real estate prices in the capital quadrupled. Major urban centers like [[Okami]], [[Toyozawa]], and [[Mito]] experienced unprecedented construction booms. The government's aggressive stimulus spending on public works, including the expansion of the [[Okami Metro|Rapid Metro system]] and the [[Yagikoshu Data Corridor]] digital infrastructure initiative, contributed to the frenzied investment atmosphere.

=== Foreign policy ===
Katō continued the diplomatic normalization trend begun under Emi, strengthening ties with [[Zong]] and [[Nakamizu]] through the '''"Fosian Partnership Initiative"''' of 1988. He also signed the landmark '''Joint Declaration on Northern Cooperation''' with [[Sukoyria|the Republic of Sukoyria]] in March 1989, resolving decades of maritime boundary disputes.

=== Resignation ===
Amid growing concerns about the sustainability of the speculative boom and intensifying factional struggles within the LDP, Katō resigned on August 11, 1989, citing health reasons. He was succeeded by [[Kentaro Tokuyama]] (1989–1990), who would preside over the bubble's collapse and the beginning of the [[Lost Decades]].

== Later life ==
After his resignation, Katō remained an influential elder statesman within the LDP, serving as '''Guardian of the Party Traditions''' (a largely ceremonial role) until his retirement from active politics in 2005. He frequently warned younger politicians about the dangers of unchecked monetary expansion, though critics noted the irony given his own role in creating the bubble.

In 2016, following the election of the first Communist prime minister [[Sho Aoyagi]], Katō publicly expressed concerns about the direction of Sanese democracy, becoming one of the most prominent voices of the conservative establishment during the [[Aoyagi Era]].

Katō died of natural causes on September 3, 2020, at [[Nakajima Hospital]] in [[Okami]], surrounded by his family. He was 95 years old. His funeral was attended by numerous political figures, including then Deputy Prime Minister [[Honda Shigetsuma]], who delivered a eulogy praising Katō's "unwavering dedication to Sanese prosperity."

== Family and legacy ==
Katō married Yumiko (née Takigawa) in 1952; she predeceased him by six months in March 2020. They had three children: two daughters and one son, Katō Kenzō, who served in the [[House of Councillors (Okaiken)|House of Councillors]] from 2010–2021 representing Aizawa Prefecture.

Historians remain divided on Katō's legacy. Supporters credit him with completing Okaiken's transformation into a global economic powerhouse and modernizing the nation's infrastructure, while critics argue that his aggressive deregulation directly enabled the speculative mania that led to the catastrophic collapse of the early 1990s and the subsequent '''Lost Decades''' of economic stagnation.

